+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+To show progress while a file is read, use :func:`~rich.progress.open` in place of the builtin ``open``. It returns a context manager which displays a progress bar while the file is open, and the bar advances as bytes are read. Here's an example which reads a file as text::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.csv") as f:
+        for line in f:
+            process(line)
+
+The default mode is ``'r'``, which returns text, as the builtin ``open`` does. Use ``'rb'`` to get bytes instead::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("image.png", "rb") as f:
+        data = f.read()
+
+The ``total`` of the progress bar is the size of the file in bytes, which is looked up automatically when you open a path. The file is closed when the ``with`` block exits.
+
+If you want to use a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance you already have, call :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` on it instead. It adds a task (or uses one you pass with ``task_id``) and returns a reader without starting the display, so you can start it yourself::
+
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        with progress.open("data.csv", "rb", description="Loading") as f:
+            data = f.read()
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file object, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` to show progress while it is read. The file-like object must be binary, and you must give its size in bytes with ``total``, since it can't be looked up. Here's an example::
+
+    import os
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    size = os.path.getsize("archive.zip")
+    with open("archive.zip", "rb") as raw:
+        with wrap_file(raw, total=size) as f:
+            data = f.read()
+
+The wrapped file is not closed when the ``with`` block exits, so you still need to close it yourself, as shown above.
+
+:meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` is the method form, which attaches the reader to a Progress instance. You can pass an existing task with ``task_id``, in which case the task's total is used if you don't give ``total``::
+
+    import os
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        task_id = progress.add_task("Parsing", total=os.path.getsize("archive.zip"))
+        with open("archive.zip", "rb") as raw:
+            reader = progress.wrap_file(raw, task_id=task_id)
+            data = reader.read()
+
